Z||000020||falconhulk||11-19-2001||11:31 AM||||I bought the xbox.  Its great.  I have had no problems and dont think i will.  Xbox is technically supirior as stated earlier.  The current generation of xbox games are not representitive of the power of the system.  Developers did not get development kits until august.  What we have today are games deleloped on PC and ported.  They still look great.  halo is a blast.  Not sure what you mean "Another piece of MS crap".  I have the sidewinder joystic, sidewinder wheel and the MS mouse.  All Hardware with the MS name and it works great and has been a blast to use.  Get your head out of your ass.  Sometimes people are so "Anti-establishment" it makes me sick.  You are way to influenced my the media and web fourms.  Start thinking for yourself.  Play the system before you ***** .  For the money right now xbox is the best you can get.  it is a DVD player with 5.1 output.  You can rip cds to the HD and play them as if you had a mp3 player connected to your home entertainment center.<BR>You can also play your own music in game.  My wife even loves it.  Nintendos games have always been for kids.  I bought a N64 and liked it alot but that was 5 years ago.  since then I moved more into PC games.  The xbox will have more of the type of games played on PC as thats where most of the developers are coming from.  But it can also do console type game  fantastically.  Have you seen DOA3?  I have it its amazing.  I dont knock gamecube.  the starwars game looks amazing!  I dont like Nintendos choice to keep the proprietary format though.  the thing about DVD and CD is that they can be produced in many places but the minidvd format is very limited to where it can be produced.  This raises the cost to the developer.  Same problem n64 had with cartridge format.  I rented the PS2 the day it came out.  I just did not like it.  It was not fun.  I have been playing video games for 20 years now, long enough to know that  fun, graphicall supiror system does not always win (ie dreamcast).  It will be a real shame if this system fails.  it really is fantastic.  ||||reg||1
Z||000021||p4n1c||11-19-2001||12:06 PM||||If you believe Bill Gates, then you really are stupid.  And yes, the graphics look no better then my GeForce2.  Why?  Because the GeForce3 is just faster core/memory speed  for a higher framerate at higher resolutions and has hardware vertex shading and a couple other tweaks.  That's it.<P>I don't care if you work for friggin' EA or own 20 game mag's.  If you believe the propangada that Microsoft always insue's with their marketing, you really are a mindless sheep.  Last time I checked, the demo unit was the exact same thing as the X-Box, same hardware.  And if you don't think the X-Box is not a PC you are sadly mistaken.  It runs a derivative of Windows CE, on an Intel platform with all off the shelf computer parts.  Look on the Microsoft <B>AND</B> nVidia site.  Bill Gates lies his way out of everything and for people to believe means that Microsoft really will win over the stupid.  <P>And you just proved my point about the damn controller issue, you said yourself X-Box controller was second to GameCube.<P>And you mindless automaton, the hard drive is only useful for patching the games as said previously, or they wouldn't have made memory cards for storing saved games, what else is 8 gigs going to be used for? Nothing, it'll be used for OS patches and game patches, just like what happens with all other Microsoft products.<P>Broadband doesn't weight 7lbs!  Dreamcast had one too that you could purchase(it was the first 128-bit system and the first system to introduce broadband, but I bet you already knew that) and it didn't weight 9lbs.<P>Sega has always had superior hardware, but never the backing of developers or fans for the hardware.<P>And ATi hardware has always been more superior.  Many people have admitted that, go look at Tom's Hardware, the drivers are usually sub-standard, because IMHO the programmers can never get anything right.  But the hardware is always exceptional.<P>If there was such a problem with compatibility with developers, why the hell have the game developer's been complaining about the PS2 and how difficult it is to program for because of the two vector units?<P>In my experience, Nintendo has always had the best games, why?  Because all of the developers have supported Nintendo systems.  The reason developers would fallback to X-Box is because the development is like that of programming games for Windows(What is that?  It's not like a PC?  Bullsh**).<P>If you think a broadband adaptor has anything to do with graphics, then you should read up on game programming.  You can have a 56k modem and crank the graphics, why?  Because all the graphics processing is down on the client side, not the server side.  Lag happens because of latency of the connection.  If any game is written to send graphics over it, then the developer is a retard.  Network gaming works by sending client states to the server, not graphics!  The latency will increase for the amount of information in the client state or the amount of load the server is taking.  This is why client side prediction was implemented, so that the client could guess at where the players in the gaming world would be next to lighten load on the server.  This is why, as an example, Quakeworld works much better than playing regular Quake online.<P>I love the Sega and Nintendo systems, Neogeo too.  I think some of the games on PS and PS2 are pretty sweet too, but I will not fall into the mindless marketing that Microsoft always puts out to cover the useless, buggy crap they produce.  And developers have already said that some of the games will be released on PC.||||reg||1
Z||000022||btoth||11-19-2001||12:21 PM||||My only problem with Nintendo is all the stupid cartoon graphics.  They even say that they're target audience is young kids.<P>My N64 was the biggest waste of money.  Zelda was the only game worth playing and it's graphics were still inferior to most computer games.  And all the games on that system look the same.  Same 3D worlds, same bad camera angles.  Nintendo has never seemed to use it's technology to its potential.<P>X-Box definatly has some cool games, and most were originally (and most still are) comming to the PC.  So.... why not buy a PC?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Playstation, I never liked all the jagged lines and weird problems with their 3D engines. (Like walls disapearing when you got too close to them.)  Playstation 2 seems to have fixed most of that.<P>I guess time will tell... but I'm not going to buy any of them.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I'll stick to computers.||||reg||1
Z||000023||p4n1c||11-19-2001||12:29 PM||||Amen brother.  I'm sticking to the computer, unless I find a really cool console that I don't get bored of after a couple weeks.<P>I can upgrade my computer whenever I want to keep up with the cool games being released.<P>And Halo was originally to be released on PC and the only reason games are being released for X-Box that were originally for PC is because a) it's easy to port because you can just get the development kit for VC++ because it's a damn computer and b) they needed somewhat decent games for launch.<P>Nintendo has said that they are not solely aiming at the young audience for the GameCube either.<P>I have a friend with a GameCube and that makes me happy, because I don't have to buy one.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
